('OVERLAY','1825 / THE FORT BEGINS',"In eighteen twenty-five, the London based Hudson's Bay Company established Fort Vancouver as the headquarters of its interior fur trade. The location was on the north bank of the Columbia River, in what is now Washington. These illustrated timber walls suggest a trading post. They are not an exact reconstruction of the original fort."),
('OVERLAY','A WORKING COMMUNITY',"The real place was more than a wall around a few buildings. It served a network of trading posts, with workers including carpenters, blacksmiths, bakers, trappers and traders. The village could grow to hundreds of people when fur brigades arrived. That wider community gives the small scene on the coin a much larger story."),
('OVERLAY','THE PORTRAIT AND THE DATES',"Now look at the portrait side in our photograph. The name below the bust identifies Doctor John McLoughlin. He was the fort's chief factor, responsible for its operations, from eighteen twenty-five to eighteen forty-five. Beside him, eighteen twenty-five and nineteen twenty-five mark the hundred year anniversary. The holder label reads P-C-G-S, M-S sixty-three."),
